\begin_layout Title
Problem Set 1: From Two-Good Demand to Verified General Demand
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
ECON 803 - Microeconomic Theory I - Fall 2026
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
Student edition - questions only
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The set has two parts. Complete Part I by hand before using a coding or formal-proof assistant. In Part II, you may use Codex, but you must inspect and validate the resulting artifacts. No due date is stated in this source; use the date announced in class.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This benchmark workshop draws from textbook Problems 1.1, 1.2, and 1.4. The authoritative Chapter 1 problem section remains in the book and ends with Problem 1.5, the household-demand use case.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Part I. By hand
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The three board problems use numbers chosen to keep the arithmetic transparent. For each one, show the derivation and state the economic point in one sentence.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
1. Nominal scaling is not a real change
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let \begin_inset Formula $X=\mathbb R_+^2$\end_inset
 and \begin_inset Formula $B(p,w)=\{x\in X:p\cdot x\leq w\}$\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(a) Prove directly that \begin_inset Formula $B(\lambda p,\lambda w)=B(p,w)$\end_inset
 for every \begin_inset Formula $\lambda>0$\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(b) Explain why any demand correspondence generated by maximizing a fixed preference relation is homogeneous of degree zero in \begin_inset Formula $(p,w)$\end_inset
. State whether continuity, concavity, or uniqueness is needed for this conclusion.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(c) At \begin_inset Formula $p=(1,2)$\end_inset
 and \begin_inset Formula $w=12$\end_inset
, derive demand for \begin_inset Formula $u(x)=\sqrt{x_1x_2}$\end_inset
. Check the budget and then verify directly that multiplying prices and wealth by five leaves the chosen bundle unchanged.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
2. Curvature changes substitution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \[u(x)=\left(\tfrac12\sqrt{x_1}+\tfrac12\sqrt{x_2}\right)^2,\]
\end_inset

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
the equal-weight CES utility with \begin_inset Formula $\rho=1/2$\end_inset
 and \begin_inset Formula $\sigma=2$\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(a) At \begin_inset Formula $p=(1,4)$\end_inset
 and \begin_inset Formula $w=10$\end_inset
, use the tangency condition and the budget to derive demand. Compute expenditure shares.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(b) Raise \begin_inset Formula $p_2$\end_inset
 from \begin_inset Formula $4$\end_inset
 to \begin_inset Formula $9$\end_inset
, holding \begin_inset Formula $p_1=1$\end_inset
 and \begin_inset Formula $w=10$\end_inset
. Recompute demand and shares.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(c) At the original prices and wealth, compute demand for the equal-weight Cobb-Douglas utility \begin_inset Formula $u_{CD}(x)=\sqrt{x_1x_2}$\end_inset
. Explain what the comparison isolates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
3. Corners and kinks are economic, not errors
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
At \begin_inset Formula $p=(1,2)$\end_inset
 and \begin_inset Formula $w=12$\end_inset
, consider separately \begin_inset Formula $u^A(x)=2x_1+x_2$\end_inset
 and \begin_inset Formula $u^B(x)=\min\{x_1,x_2\}$\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(a) Derive each demand.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(b) For each model, identify the inequality or equality that determines the solution.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(c) Explain why applying an interior differentiable tangency condition mechanically would miss the point of both examples.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Part II. Verified scale-up with Codex and Lean
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Part II extends Problems 1 and 2. It does not replace them. Complete the bridge before opening Codex.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
4. The bridge
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Write four short answers:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Seed: Which conclusion from Problem 1 or 2 will you extend?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Fixed: Which preference, budget, and positivity assumptions remain unchanged?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Scaled: Which object changes from two goods to five goods or arbitrary finite dimension?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Risk: Which domain violation, numerical error, or logical overclaim becomes possible after scaling?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
5. Computational scale-up: five-good CES demand
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For \begin_inset Formula $L$\end_inset
 goods, normalized weights \begin_inset Formula $a_\ell>0$\end_inset
 with \begin_inset Formula $\sum_\ell a_\ell=1$\end_inset
, and elasticity \begin_inset Formula $\sigma>0$\end_inset
, use
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \[b_\ell(p)=\frac{a_\ell^\sigma p_\ell^{1-\sigma}}{\sum_k a_k^\sigma p_k^{1-\sigma}},\qquad x_\ell(p,w)=\frac{w b_\ell(p)}{p_\ell}.\]
\end_inset

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Take
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \[a=(0.10,0.15,0.20,0.25,0.30),\quad p=(1,2,3,4,5),\quad w=120,\]
\end_inset

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
and compare \begin_inset Formula $\sigma\in\{1/2,1,2\}$\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(a) Before coding, derive the displayed share formula from the CES first-order conditions and say which normalization makes it valid at \begin_inset Formula $\sigma=1$\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(b) Ask Codex to create work/ps01_ces.py. The program must return shares and quantities, validate dimensions and strict positivity, retain full precision internally, and print one readable comparison table.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(c) Add automated checks for \begin_inset Formula $\sum_\ell b_\ell=1$\end_inset
, \begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x=w$\end_inset
, homogeneity under \begin_inset Formula $(p,w)\mapsto(7p,7w)$\end_inset
, and the Cobb-Douglas share restriction at \begin_inset Formula $\sigma=1$\end_inset
. Use an explicit numerical tolerance no larger than \begin_inset Formula $10^{-10}$\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(d) Add one deliberately rejected input with a zero price. Explain why rejection, rather than a numerical answer, is the economically correct behavior.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(e) Interpret how expenditure-share dispersion changes with \begin_inset Formula $\sigma$\end_inset
. This paragraph must be written by you; a finite table is not a general proof.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A suitable first prompt is:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation
Read Problems 2 and 5 and problemsets/AGENTS.md. First restate the CES assumptions and propose a dependency-ordered plan. Then implement only the five-good scale-up in work/ps01_ces.py, add the specified identity checks and rejected input, run the file, and report the command and result. Do not write the final economic interpretation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
6. Formal scale-up: arbitrary finite dimension
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Problem 1 proved a two-good budget identity. The formal target removes the arbitrary two-good restriction while retaining the economically essential positive common scale.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(a) Read formal/Econ803/Consumer/Demand.lean and Starter.lean. Record the types of InBudget, UtilityDemand, inBudget_scale_iff, and utilityDemand_scale_iff.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(b) In your own work/PS01_ConsumerDemand.lean, prove the proposition TwoScaleClaim: for arbitrary finite \begin_inset Formula $L$\end_inset
, utility \begin_inset Formula $u$\end_inset
, bundle \begin_inset Formula $x$\end_inset
, price vector \begin_inset Formula $p$\end_inset
, wealth \begin_inset Formula $w$\end_inset
, and positive scalars \begin_inset Formula $a,b$\end_inset
, the same bundle is demanded at \begin_inset Formula $(ap,aw)$\end_inset
 if and only if it is demanded at \begin_inset Formula $(bp,bw)$\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(c) Do not use sorry, admit, a new axiom, or a numerical specialization. Ask Codex first for the dependency map, then for a proof using the existing one-scale theorem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(d) From formal/, validate with
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
\size scriptsize
lake env lean ../problemsets/ps01_consumer_demand/work/PS01_ConsumerDemand.lean
\size default
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Run #print axioms on your completed theorem and report the output separately from compilation success.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(e) Explain why this theorem needs neither differentiability nor concavity, and why it does not by itself prove budget exhaustion. Name the additional economic assumptions used by the book's budget-exhaustion theorem.
\end_layout

